---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { EVIDENCE_DESCRIPTIONS, EVIDENCE_LABELS, EVIDENCE_LEVELS, SITE } from '../config/site';
---

<BaseLayout title="Editorial Policy" description={`How ${SITE.name} sources, reviews, and labels its reporting.`}>
  <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>

  <h2>How AI is used in producing this publication</h2>
  <p>
    AI may help discover sources and prepare first drafts. It does not decide what gets published. A human editor
    checks and approves every article before it goes live, and nothing generated or imported by AI publishes
    automatically — every change reaches the live site only through a reviewed, human-approved pull request merged
    to <code>main</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because of that, articles here carry a house byline ("{SITE.defaultAuthor}") instead of a person's name. It
    would be misleading to put an individual editor's name on AI-drafted copy as though they wrote it themselves.
    The "Reviewed by" credit on each article is the real, accountable human who checked it before it went live.
  </p>

  <h2>Evidence labels</h2>
  <p>Every article carries one of three evidence labels, shown on its card and at the top of the article:</p>
  <dl>
    {
      EVIDENCE_LEVELS.map((level) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{EVIDENCE_LABELS[level]}</dt>
          <dd>{EVIDENCE_DESCRIPTIONS[level]}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2>Effort to try</h2>
  <p>
    Every article's sidebar carries an effort rating: <strong>Quick</strong>, <strong>Moderate</strong>, or
    <strong>Involved</strong>. It's about time investment, not skill, a tool you already know well might be "Quick"
    for you even if we rated it "Moderate" for a typical reader coming to it cold.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you use it</h2>
  <p>
    Every article's sidebar includes a "Before you use it" checklist covering client data handling, usage rights,
    disclosure obligations, accessibility, and cost, alongside the tools it discusses and (for Test and Workflow
    articles) a quick summary. Any field we haven't personally checked is marked
    <code>not verified</code> rather than guessed at.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections and withdrawals</h2>
  <p>
    When new information changes our assessment, we update the article and bump its "updated" date; if we re-check
    the underlying facts without changing the conclusion, we bump "last verified" instead. When something we
    reported turns out to be wrong, no longer applies, or is withdrawn by its maker, we withdraw the article: its
    URL keeps working, but the page shows a short withdrawal notice explaining why instead of the original body. A
    withdrawn article is removed from listings, search, and our RSS feed, and marked so search engines don't index
    it.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
